RPC bulk reservation can self-throttle object reads
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/2f903dbb63188191bb4643c6cc90c06a?sev=&repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit: 6c0e8481dad3757eaa3a3e5d1befb01a2c406093
Commit link: https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/6c0e8481dad3757eaa3a3e5d1befb01a2c406093

The original report is stale in class names after the storage RPC admission
split, but the underlying low-limit self-throttle was still valid: a remote
object read can hold a read-handle session permit while issuing a shard read.
With an admission limit of 2, the newer `Read` class had only one shared read
slot, so the held read-handle permit could block the following `ShardRead` or
`ShardReadRange` even though total admission capacity was not exhausted.

The fix rejects unsafe public configuration values instead of trying to support
pathological test-only limits. `ARGMIN_STORAGE_NODE_RPC_ADMISSION_LIMIT` now
has a minimum of 8, exposed as
`LocalUnixStorageNodeClientConfig::MIN_RPC_ADMISSION_LIMIT`. That floor gives
room for a held read-handle session plus the nested shard read, while preserving
reserved control/completion capacity and leaving headroom for list, progress,
and start-write classes. Low-level unit tests may still construct smaller
admission gates directly when intentionally testing exhaustion behavior.

Regression coverage added:
- `storage_node_rpc_admission_limit_below_minimum` verifies env config rejects
  values below 8.
- `unix_storage_node_rpc_admission_minimum_supports_nested_read_and_mixed_work`
  verifies the configured minimum admits the representative nested read plus
  list, progress, start-write, completion, and control work.

Verification run before the fix commit:
- `cargo fmt`
- `cargo nextest run -p argmin-s3 storage_node_rpc_admission`
- `cargo nextest run -p storage unix_storage_node_rpc_admission`
- `cargo nextest run -p argmin-s3`
- `cargo nextest run -p storage`
- `git diff --check`

